content.text:
The compliance review caught six real gaps:

- legacy bootstrap still treated an installed Git hook as consent;
- session evidence accepted any phase except `"ended"`;
- `null` PII categories became `false`, weakening redaction defaults;
- `null` scalar fields were accepted as valid hook settings;
- the cancellation test never actually waited on the route lock;
- directory sync was not Windows-safe, and registry-path ownership was still duplicated.

These are all Task 2 contract issues, so I’m fixing them before any quality review.

The quality pass found four production issues plus two correctness hardening items:

- a genuine check-then-open race in settings/legacy provenance;
- registry directory creation was not itself crash-durable;
- legacy metadata could spawn one Git process per candidate;
- Unix permission assertions would fail on native Windows;
- null maps lost nil semantics;
- future PII fields were not exhaustively classified.

I’m sending the full set back now. The provenance fix must bind validation to the same opened file—not merely add another pre-read check.

The required red phase is complete:

- both removed flags are still present;
- wizard/help/user-facing strings still advertise global activation;
- linked-worktree uninstall leaves the wrong marker and deletes shared hooks despite an active sibling.

The no-user-hook-removal test already passes, which pins an invariant we must preserve. An explicit-directory read-only classifier was added to make sibling evaluation possible without changing process CWD.

